window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_main-5eb70c6723f66380"]={"key":"ny_main-5eb70c6723f66380","group":"ny_main","uid":"5eb70c6723f66380","rank":96,"title":"ballantry candlewick cross jurisdiction complaints policy","occupation":"lawyers","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_main","prompt":"You’re the senior regulatory lawyer supporting Ballantry Practice as it absorbs Candlewick Foundry’s complaints function this week. Rosalind Laurent has authorised corrections before the combined public complaints policy is published on Friday, and Jasmin Penrose needs the result to work at the front line without exposing information that should remain restricted. Tadeo Mortensen’s draft says every complaint record is kept for seven years from opening and may be shared with any Ballantry or Candlewick entity or regulator whenever useful. That wording cannot go forward without a legally defensible publication decision. Part of the audience will be regulatory lawyers, but most readers will be clients, other complainants, and intake staff with no regulatory background; keep it precise without turning it into a privacy-law or complaints-handling tutorial. There is only one lawyer-day left and no file-by-file legacy audit can be completed before publication. The verified annual inventory is limited to this: for the 12 months ended 31 August 2024, England and Wales received 80 complaints, with 68 closed at first response and 12 externally escalated, while Ontario received 50, with 44 closed at first response and 6 externally escalated; for the 12 months ended 31 August 2025, the corresponding figures were 100, 78 and 22 for England and Wales and 60, 48 and 12 for Ontario. Planning’s stated integration assumption is that next-period volumes remain at the latest annual level and the first-response closure rate improves by exactly five percentage points in each jurisdiction. Four legacy examples also need a defensible treatment without adding missing facts: EW-22 concerns an act dated 15 February 2019 and a final response dated 20 March 2023; EW-23 concerns an act dated 15 May 2023 and a final response dated 20 July 2023; ON-19 completed on 30 June 2019 and closed on 15 December 2019, contains a client-identification record and trust ledger, has no known hold, and has no verified consent status; ON-25 closed on 10 February 2025 after a threatened negligence claim and remains subject to a preservation notice with no release date. Prepare `corrected_cross_jurisdiction_public_complaints_policy.docx` and write it at the top level of the working directory using exactly that filename. The document must be no more than seven pages in total and must: (1) provide publication-ready public policy text for England and Wales and Ontario, applying the law and professional rules in force as at 30 September 2025 and defining “client” before first use; (2) distinguish clients, other complainants, regulators, service providers, and group entities where their rights or permitted access differ; (3) correct the retention and disclosure language, address incomplete legacy classifications, and give Jasmin’s team instructions concrete enough to use without asking the author; (4) include a compact internal decision table applying the date-sensitive rules to the four legacy examples without revealing unnecessary case detail in the public text; (5) compare the two annual periods, explain the material changes without claiming unsupported causes, and show the planning projection separately from current requirements and observed results; (6) identify the legal and regulatory authorities relied on, while keeping technical exposition proportionate for public readers; and (7) include a concise change record and an internal approval note after a clear page break. Do not invent additional cases, dates, volumes, assumptions, consents, holds, or legal outcomes. Your authority is limited to the legal and operational facts and the publication decision, not the merits of any complaint or remedial action in an individual matter. Finish with exactly one recommendation on whether the corrected package should be published on Friday, and make that recommendation the last substantive sentence rather than ending with a list of next steps.\n\n---\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `corrected_cross_jurisdiction_public_complaints_policy.docx` — A publication-ready cross-jurisdiction public complaints policy with an internal legacy-record decision table, change record, comparative analysis, and publication approval note. (Write the file at the top level of the working directory using exactly the specified filename.; The complete document must not exceed seven rendered pages.; Apply only England and Wales and Ontario requirements in force as at 30 September 2025.; Separate publication-ready text from internal decision material with a clear page break.; End with exactly one publication recommendation as the last substantive sentence.; Do not invent facts, figures, assumptions, consents, holds, or case outcomes.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/corrected_cross_jurisdiction_public_complaints_policy.docx","bytes":48254,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/8c5351f74c0a396a5fa5a094ecad818c39e019f1a10a7a90a3cf3a9968d9334e.docx","sha256":"8c5351f74c0a396a5fa5a094ecad818c39e019f1a10a7a90a3cf3a9968d9334e","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"Cross-Jurisdiction Complaints and Records Policy\nEngland and Wales and Ontario | Public policy | Legal position current as at 30 September 2025\nSCOPE  This policy applies only to complaint handling and complaint-record controls in England and Wales and Ontario. It does not state the law of any other jurisdiction. It applies to the integrated function, including transferred records, without changing any existing confidentiality, privilege, purpose restriction, or hold.\n\n1. Definitions and who may use the process\nTerm\nMeaning and practical effect\nClient\nThe person or entity that instructs Ballantry Practice to provide legal services. A beneficiary, family member, employee, representative, or other service recipient is not automatically a client unless that person or entity is the instructing party.\nOther complainant\nA person who raises a concern but is not the client. They may use the intake route and may be able to use an external route under its own rules, but this does not give access to a client's confidential or privileged information.\nRegulator; group entity; service provider\nA regulator acts under its statutory or professional remit. A Ballantry or Candlewick group entity receives no automatic file access. A service provider may handle material only for a defined service under written safeguards and continuing practice accountability.\n\n2. Making a complaint\nA complaint may be raised with the Complaints Intake Team through the practice's published complaints contact route, in writing, by telephone, or in another reasonable accessible format. Tell us how to contact you, what happened, the relevant matter if known, and the outcome sought; we will not reject a concern merely because every detail is not available.\nAt receipt, the team records the jurisdiction, complainant status, key dates, the material needed to investigate, and any restriction or preservation flag. The team acknowledges and routes the concern, keeps the person informed, and does not decide merits or disclose a file outside the controls in this policy.\nA client receives, in writing at engagement and on request, the right to complain, how and to whom to complain within the practice, and the relevant external routes. Accessible communication will be used where reasonably required.\n3. England and Wales\nWe handle complaints promptly, fairly, and free of charge.\nA service concern is considered through our procedure first. Where it remains unresolved after eight weeks, we provide written information about the Legal Ombudsman route. The Legal Ombudsman deals with eligible service complaints; the Solicitors Regulation Authority (SRA) regulates professional conduct. A concern about conduct should be directed or reported to the SRA rather than treated solely as a Legal Ombudsman service complaint. Some concerns may require both routes.\nFor a matter within the Legal Ombudsman Scheme Rules, the current outer rule is generally one year from the act or omission, or one year from when the complainant should reasonably have known there was cause to complain. The complaint must also generally be made within six months of the practice's compliant final response. The Legal Ombudsman may extend time where it is fair and reasonable. We provide the applicable route information in the final response; historical matters are assessed under the rule applicable at the relevant date.\nWe keep current and former clients' affairs confidential unless disclosure is required or permitted by law or the client consents. A concern from another complainant does not change that duty. A lawful regulatory request is assessed for scope, authority, and necessity before material is released.\n4. Ontario\nA lawyer has a continuing duty to hold client information in strict confidence unless authorised by the client or otherwise permitted or required by law or professional rules. Any person, including a person who is not a client, may raise a concern with the Law Society of Ontario; that route does not confer a right to another person's confidential file.\nPIPEDA applies to personal information handled in commercial activities in Ontario, subject to its statutory scope and exceptions. The practice remains accountable for complaint information. We do not infer consent from silence, from an old retainer, or merely from the existence of a complaint. A disclosure without consent requires an applicable statutory or professional exception and is limited to what is necessary for its stated purpose.\nWe cooperate with lawful Law Society of Ontario processes. That duty is not authority to publish a complaint record or circulate it freely within the combined group. Access and correction requests are considered case by case, with protection for solicitor-client privilege, confidential third-party information, and other lawful refusal grounds.\n5. Information, sharing, and access\nControl\nPublic commitment\nPurpose and minimisation\nFor United Kingdom complaint information, processing is for specified purposes: receiving, investigating, responding, meeting legal or professional obligations, handling a claim, and service assurance. We use an appropriate UK GDPR lawful basis, such as legal obligation or legitimate interests; consent is not the universal basis. We collect and disclose only complaint information that is adequate, relevant, and limited to what is necessary.\nSensitive material and requests\nHealth, equality, criminal-offence, or similarly sensitive complaint material may require an additional legal condition and tighter handling. We verify identity and consider access or correction requests without promising unrestricted disclosure of privileged material, another person's information, or material covered by an applicable statutory restriction. We may redact, withhold, or explain a lawful refusal.\nRecipients and group access\nDisclosure to the Legal Ombudsman, SRA, Law Society of Ontario, courts, insurers, or another authorised recipient needs a valid legal or professional basis and only the information reasonably necessary for that purpose. Ballantry and Candlewick entities are not a single unrestricted audience: access is role-based, need-to-know, and securely escalated before proposed sharing.\nService providers and systems\nA service provider or cross-border system may receive purpose-limited access only under contractual safeguards, appropriate security, and continuing practice accountability. Transfer or integration does not extinguish confidentiality, privilege, purpose restrictions, or holds.\n\n6. Retention, preservation, and disposal\nThere is no universal seven-year period running from complaint opening, and no single retention period governs every complaint-record category in both jurisdictions. We classify material by jurisdiction, record category, purpose, governing minimum period, trigger date, limitation or insurance need, active process, and disposition or review point. Depending on category, a trigger may be completion, creation, the end of an applicable fiscal period, or documented release of a hold. Complaint closure is not the destruction trigger for every embedded record category.\nRecord category\nRetention rule\nGeneral complaint material\nPersonal data is not retained longer than necessary for the identified purpose, subject to applicable duties and holds. In Ontario, personal information is kept only as long as necessary for identified purposes and is securely destroyed, erased, or anonymised when no longer required, subject to other record duties and holds.\nOntario prescribed client-identification and verification records\nMinimum six years following completion. This is a category-specific, completion-based rule; it is not a rule for all complaint records.\nOntario prescribed financial or trust-accounting records\nSeparate minimum ten-year treatment, generally measured from the end of the applicable fiscal period. It is not displaced by complaint closure or the identification-record rule.\n\nLegal holds, preservation notices, active proceedings, investigations, and reasonably anticipated claims suspend ordinary destruction until formally released. Where a legacy classification, consent, closure status, or governing date cannot be verified, we apply restricted preservation and escalation, not assumed disclosure or routine destruction. Any disposal must be authorised and recorded with what was destroyed or anonymised, the rule applied, when it occurred, and who authorised it.\n7. Frontline controls\nWhen\nOwner\nRequired action\nOn receipt\nComplaints Intake Team\nRecord the minimum needed for routing; identify jurisdiction and the status of the person raising the concern; restrict the file.\nBefore disclosure or destruction\nRegulatory lawyer, privacy lead, and records owner\nVerify authority, necessity, category, trigger, privilege/confidentiality, and any hold. Escalate uncertainty before release or disposal.\nHold release and review\nLitigation counsel; then records owner\nOnly a documented formal release ends a hold. Review this integrated policy and record schedule at least annually and before a material legal or regulatory change.\n\nINTERNAL APPROVAL NOTE AND CONTROLLED ANNEX\nNot public policy. Restricted to publication approvers, intake leadership, records, privacy, and regulatory legal teams.\nDECISION BOUNDARY  This note supports the publication decision only. It does not decide the merits of a complaint, any remedy, or any individual legal outcome. The original blanket retention and sharing propositions are not used in the corrected package; unresolved legacy records remain controlled under the published transitional rules.\n\nObserved inventory: results, not legal requirements\nPeriod\nE&W\nreceived\nE&W\nfirst\nE&W\nescalated\nON\nreceived\nON\nfirst\nON\nescalated\nCombined\nreceived / first / escalated\n12 months ended 31 Aug 2024\n80\n68\n12\n50\n44\n6\n130 / 112 / 18\n12 months ended 31 Aug 2025\n100\n78\n22\n60\n48\n12\n160 / 126 / 34\n\nObserved change: total complaints rose from 130 to 160 (+30; 23.1%). England and Wales rose by 20 (25.0%); Ontario rose by 10 (20.0%). External escalations rose from 18 to 34 (+16; 88.9%).\nEscalation rates changed from 15.0% to 22.0% in England and Wales (+7.0pp) and from 12.0% to 20.0% in Ontario (+8.0pp). Combined first-response closure fell from 86.2% to 78.8% (-7.4pp); jurisdiction closure rates were 85.0% to 78.0% and 88.0% to 80.0%. The inventory shows higher volume and a materially higher escalation share; it does not establish why either changed.\nPlanning projection: separate from observed results and current requirements\nJurisdiction\nVolume\n2025 first-response rate\nStated change\nProjected rate\nProjected first\nProjected escalations\nEngland and Wales\n100\n78.0%\n+5.0pp\n83.0%\n83\n17\nOntario\n60\n80.0%\n+5.0pp\n85.0%\n51\n9\nCombined\n160\n78.8%\nDerived\n83.8%\n134\n26\n\nPlanning holds volumes at the latest annual level and adds exactly 5.0 percentage points to each jurisdiction's observed first-response closure rate. It is an integration assumption, not an observed result, service promise, retention rule, or legal requirement.\nLegacy record decision table\nMatter\nKnown governing date / uncertainty\nApplicable rule\nPresent disposition at 30 Sep 2025\nEscalation or review trigger\nEW-22\nAct: 15 Feb 2019. Final response: 20 Mar 2023, before 1 Apr 2023.\nApply the former Legal Ombudsman outer framework: six years from act or omission, or three years from when the complainant should reasonably have known there was cause to complain, plus six months from a compliant final response. Do not replace that historical rule with the later one-year framework.\nRestricted preservation and category review. An Ombudsman window does not itself authorise destruction.\nRegulatory lawyer verifies final-response compliance, applicable historical routing, holds, limitation and insurance needs before any retention decision.\nEW-23\nAct: 15 May 2023. Final response: 20 Jul 2023, after 1 Apr 2023.\nApply the post-1 April 2023 Legal Ombudsman framework: generally one year from act or omission, or one year from reasonable knowledge, plus six months from a compliant final response; the Ombudsman retains fair-and-reasonable discretion.\nKeep eligibility analysis separate from retention. Restricted preservation and category review; no disposal based only on complaint timing.\nRegulatory lawyer checks route information, any hold, and record-category retention factors before an authorised disposition.\nON-19\nCompleted: 30 Jun 2019. Closed: 15 Dec 2019. Client-identification record and trust ledger. No known hold. Consent status: unknown.\nIdentification/verification minimum: six years following completion; minimum elapsed on 30 Jun 2025, subject to other purposes and controls. Trust ledger: separate ten-year financial-record rule measured by the applicable fiscal-period trigger; complaint closure does not set its disposal date.\nDo not dispose of the file as one unit. Restrict the trust ledger. Consent is unknown and is not a disclosure basis; no group or regulator sharing without a validated legal or professional basis and necessity.\nRecords owner and privacy/regulatory lawyer verify category, fiscal trigger, privilege/confidentiality, insurance or other need, and then log any authorised record-level disposition.\nON-25\nClosed: 10 Feb 2025. Threatened negligence claim. Preservation notice has no release date.\nA preservation notice and reasonably anticipated claim suspend ordinary destruction until formally released; closure does not override the notice.\nContinue restricted preservation. No ordinary destruction while the notice lacks a documented release.\nLitigation counsel supplies a documented formal release; then the records owner classifies and applies the schedule.\n\nChange record\nCorrection\nCorrected position\nRetention\nReplaces a universal seven-year period from opening with jurisdiction, category, purpose, minimum, trigger, limitation/insurance, hold, and review controls.\nDisclosure\nRemoves sharing whenever useful. Requires valid legal or professional authority, necessity, role-based group access, and secure escalation.\nRoutes and dates\nStates current Legal Ombudsman one-year act-or-knowledge and six-month final-response rules; preserves the 1 April 2023 transition for earlier final responses.\nPeople and uncertainty\nDefines client as the instructing person or entity; separates other complainants; unknown legacy classification, consent, closure, or date leads to restricted preservation and escalation.\nEvidence and planning\nSeparates observed 2024/2025 inventory from the five-percentage-point planning projection and states that the inventory does not establish cause.\n\nAuthorities and control basis\nStatus\nAuthority relied on\nBinding / operative\nSRA Code of Conduct for Firms, para. 8.2 (complaints information and prompt, fair, free handling); SRA Transparency Rules, r. 3; SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, RELs and RFLs, para. 6.3 (confidentiality); Legal Ombudsman Scheme Rules, r. 4 (current rules from 1 April 2023 and prior rules for pre-change matters).\nBinding / operative\nUK GDPR arts. 5, 6, 9, 10 and 32; Data Protection Act 2018, Sch. 2 Pt. 4 (legal professional privilege and related restrictions).\nBinding / operative\nLaw Society of Ontario Rules of Professional Conduct, r. 3.3-1; PIPEDA ss. 4, 5 and 7 and Schedule 1 clauses 4.1, 4.3, 4.4, 4.5, 4.7 and 4.9; Law Society of Ontario By-Law 7.1 (identification and verification records) and By-Law 9 (financial and trust-accounting records).\nOfficial guidance\nOfficial SRA, Legal Ombudsman, Information Commissioner's Office, Office of the Privacy Commissioner of Canada, and Law Society of Ontario guidance assists application but does not replace the binding duties above.\n\nApproval analysis\nThe public text is suitable for release because it corrects the two blanket propositions, gives a usable intake and escalation route, confines legal scope to the two stated jurisdictions, and preserves category-specific controls. The absence of a file-level legacy audit does not require public release of uncertain material or routine disposal: the transitional fallback restricts, preserves, and escalates each unverified legacy issue until a competent owner verifies it.\nThe observed inventory supports operational attention to increased volume and escalation share, while the projection remains expressly separate from the current legal requirements and observed outcomes. Publication authority remains limited to this policy package and does not determine any complaint's merits, redress, or individual legal outcome.\nRecommendation: Publish the corrected package on Friday, with the transitional controls and restricted-preservation treatment set out in this note.","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"R01","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The required file exists at the submission root with the exact filename `corrected_cross_jurisdiction_public_complaints_policy.docx`.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Submitted file listing.","grader_hint":"Check the top-level submission directory and exact spelling, including the `.docx` extension."},{"rubric_item_id":"R02","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deliverable is a valid, openable DOCX whose substantive text, tables, headers, and notes can be inspected.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"DOCX package and rendered document.","grader_hint":"Open the file in a compatible office application and confirm it is not corrupt, empty, or merely a renamed non-DOCX file."},{"rubric_item_id":"R03","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The complete rendered document is no more than seven pages.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered page count.","grader_hint":"Render with standard office software using the document's embedded page settings; the tolerance is zero pages beyond the seven-page cap."},{"rubric_item_id":"R04","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Publication-ready policy text is clearly separated from internal material, with the approval note beginning after a page break and not appearing as public policy content.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Section boundaries and page flow.","grader_hint":"Confirm that a public reader could receive the policy portion without confusing the legacy table, change record, or approval analysis for public-facing terms."},{"rubric_item_id":"R05","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document states that its legal position is current as at 30 September 2025.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Policy date or legal-basis statement.","grader_hint":"Look for the full as-of date in the substantive document, not only in file metadata."},{"rubric_item_id":"R06","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The stated legal scope is confined to England and Wales and Ontario and does not imply that the policy states the law of other jurisdictions.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scope, applicability, and qualification language.","grader_hint":"Check both the opening scope and any groupwide language for jurisdictional overreach."},{"rubric_item_id":"R07","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"“Client” is defined before its first substantive use, and the definition identifies the instructing person or entity rather than silently extending the term to everyone who receives or benefits from services.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Definitions and first occurrence of the term.","grader_hint":"Search for the first use of “client,” then verify that an operative definition appears before it and handles the ambiguity identified in the brief."},{"rubric_item_id":"R08","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The policy consistently distinguishes a client from another complainant or service beneficiary where complaint access, confidentiality, or redress rights may differ.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Definitions, eligibility language, routes for complaints, and disclosure provisions.","grader_hint":"Award credit only if the distinction affects operative wording and is not merely listed in a glossary."},{"rubric_item_id":"R09","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The public-facing text uses plain, direct language and proportionate explanations without becoming a general tutorial on privacy, limitation periods, or professional regulation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Public policy prose.","grader_hint":"Assess whether an ordinary complainant can understand the policy while technical background is limited to what is needed to use it."},{"rubric_item_id":"R10","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Legally significant qualifications, dates, duties, and exceptions remain precise enough for review by legally trained readers.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Legal rules, qualifications, and citations.","grader_hint":"Check that simplification has not converted conditional rules into absolutes or merged distinct regulators and legal bases."},{"rubric_item_id":"R11","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Frontline staff can determine what to record, restrict, route, disclose, preserve, or escalate without asking the author for an unstated decision rule.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Operational clauses and escalation language.","grader_hint":"Test the policy against a new complaint, a regulator request, an uncertain legacy file, and a hold; the immediate handling route should be clear in each case."},{"rubric_item_id":"R12","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Public-facing text avoids names, detailed allegations, or other unnecessary identifying facts from the four legacy examples.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Public policy and internal case references.","grader_hint":"The internal identifiers may be used in restricted material, but the public policy should not reproduce unnecessary case facts."},{"rubric_item_id":"R13","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The internal approval note contains exactly one publication recommendation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Approval-note conclusion.","grader_hint":"Count substantive recommended dispositions, not explanatory supporting sentences; there must be one and only one."},{"rubric_item_id":"R14","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The single recommendation supports publishing the corrected package on Friday while rejecting the draft’s blanket retention and sharing propositions and preserving controlled treatment of unresolved legacy records.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommendation and supporting analysis.","grader_hint":"The defensible decision is neither publication of the defective wording nor an unnecessary delay for a complete legacy audit; it must depend on the corrected policy and transitional safeguards."},{"rubric_item_id":"R15","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The publication recommendation is the last substantive sentence in the document, with no later next-steps list or competing conclusion.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Final substantive page and sentence.","grader_hint":"Ignore purely administrative footers or signature placeholders, but treat later action lists or qualifications as substantive."},{"rubric_item_id":"R16","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document uses only the supplied cases, dates, volumes, and planning assumption and clearly marks unknown facts rather than filling them in.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Tables, calculations, case analysis, and assumptions.","grader_hint":"Compare all factual inputs with the request; derived arithmetic is allowed, but new factual predicates or assumptions are not."},{"rubric_item_id":"R17","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Observed historical results, current legal requirements, legacy date-sensitive conclusions, and planning projections are visibly distinguished.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Labels, tables, notes, and narrative.","grader_hint":"A reader should not mistake an operational target or projection for a legal requirement or an observed result."},{"rubric_item_id":"R18","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"For England and Wales, the policy states that complaints must be handled promptly, fairly, and free of charge.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"England and Wales complaint-handling clause.","grader_hint":"Accept equivalent wording only if all three concepts are explicit."},{"rubric_item_id":"R19","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The England and Wales section accurately states the written information that a client must receive about the practice’s complaint route and external redress.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Client-information and engagement-stage provisions.","grader_hint":"Look for the right to complain, how and to whom to complain, and the relevant external routes, without requiring system-level detail."},{"rubric_item_id":"R20","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The England and Wales section states the eight-week point after which an unresolved service complaint requires written information about the Legal Ombudsman route.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Complaint response timetable.","grader_hint":"The eight-week period and its consequence must both be stated."},{"rubric_item_id":"R21","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The policy states the Legal Ombudsman requirement to complain within six months of the practice’s compliant final response.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"External-redress time-limit wording.","grader_hint":"Credit requires both the six-month period and its connection to a final response that gives the required information."},{"rubric_item_id":"R22","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The current Legal Ombudsman outer time rule is accurately expressed as generally one year from the act or omission or one year from when the complainant should reasonably have known there was cause to complain, subject to the Ombudsman’s discretion.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Current England and Wales time-limit clause.","grader_hint":"Reject an unqualified one-year absolute or a statement that omits the knowledge limb."},{"rubric_item_id":"R23","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document identifies 1 April 2023 as the relevant change date for the Legal Ombudsman time rules and does not retroactively rewrite the rule applicable to a final response issued before that date.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Legal change note and EW-22/EW-23 treatment.","grader_hint":"Check that the legacy analysis aligns each matter to the time regime applicable at the relevant date rather than applying only the current public wording."},{"rubric_item_id":"R24","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The policy explains when a concern about professional conduct should be directed or reported to the Solicitors Regulation Authority rather than treated solely as a Legal Ombudsman service complaint.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"England and Wales escalation routes.","grader_hint":"The policy need not catalogue misconduct, but it must provide an intelligible distinction and route."},{"rubric_item_id":"R25","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The respective functions of the practice, the Legal Ombudsman, and the Solicitors Regulation Authority are not conflated.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Complaint process and regulator descriptions.","grader_hint":"Check that the Ombudsman is not described as the conduct regulator and that the SRA is not presented as the routine service-remedy body."},{"rubric_item_id":"R26","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The England and Wales public text provides the substance expected of a published complaints procedure, including how to complain and how and when external routes become available.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Public complaint procedure.","grader_hint":"Assess substantive usability and compliance rather than the presence of a particular heading."},{"rubric_item_id":"R27","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The England and Wales section states the duty to keep current and former clients’ affairs confidential unless disclosure is required or permitted by law or the client consents.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Confidentiality clause.","grader_hint":"Equivalent precise wording is acceptable; a generic promise to protect privacy is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R28","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"UK complaint-record processing is tied to specified purposes and an appropriate UK GDPR lawful basis rather than treating consent as the universal basis.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"England and Wales privacy and records provisions.","grader_hint":"Look for purpose limitation and context-appropriate bases such as legal obligation or legitimate interests, with consent used only where appropriate."},{"rubric_item_id":"R29","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The policy requires collection and disclosure of only complaint information that is adequate, relevant, and limited to what is necessary.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Data-minimisation language.","grader_hint":"Accept a faithful plain-language equivalent of the minimisation principle."},{"rubric_item_id":"R30","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The policy states that personal data is not retained longer than necessary for the identified purpose, subject to applicable duties and holds.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Storage-limitation and retention clauses.","grader_hint":"The principle must be operationally qualified; a bare statement that data is kept securely does not satisfy it."},{"rubric_item_id":"R31","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The England and Wales section recognises that health, equality, criminal-offence, or similarly sensitive complaint material may require an additional legal condition and tighter handling.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Sensitive-information provisions.","grader_hint":"Award credit for a concise operational treatment; an exhaustive Article 9 or Article 10 tutorial is unnecessary."},{"rubric_item_id":"R32","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Access rights are described without promising unrestricted disclosure of privileged material, another person’s information, or material covered by an applicable statutory restriction.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Access and correction provisions.","grader_hint":"The policy should preserve the ability to review, redact, withhold, or explain a refusal on a valid legal basis."},{"rubric_item_id":"R33","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Disclosure to the Legal Ombudsman, SRA, courts, insurers, or other authorised recipients is limited to a valid legal or professional basis and the information reasonably necessary for that purpose.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Permitted-disclosure clauses.","grader_hint":"Check that naming a regulator or insurer is not treated as automatic authority to send the whole complaint file."},{"rubric_item_id":"R34","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Ballantry and Candlewick entities are not treated as a single unrestricted audience; internal access is role-based and need-to-know, with secure escalation for proposed sharing.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Group-sharing, security, and access-control provisions.","grader_hint":"Look for an operative restriction that directly corrects the draft’s groupwide sharing proposition."},{"rubric_item_id":"R35","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The Ontario section states the lawyer’s continuing duty to hold client information in strict confidence unless authorised by the client or otherwise permitted or required by law or professional rules.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Ontario confidentiality clause.","grader_hint":"A generic privacy promise is insufficient; the professional confidentiality rule and its limited exceptions must be apparent."},{"rubric_item_id":"R36","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The Ontario section accurately frames PIPEDA in relation to personal information handled in commercial activities and maintains organisational accountability for complaint information.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Ontario privacy-law scope and accountability provisions.","grader_hint":"Do not award credit for an unqualified assertion that Ontario has a general private-sector provincial privacy statute equivalent to the UK GDPR."},{"rubric_item_id":"R37","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The Ontario section does not infer consent from silence, from an old retainer, or merely from the existence of a complaint.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Consent and disclosure language.","grader_hint":"Look for an express treatment of absent or unverified consent that can be applied to ON-19."},{"rubric_item_id":"R38","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Any Ontario disclosure without consent is tied to an applicable statutory or professional exception and is limited to what is necessary for the stated purpose.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Ontario permitted-disclosure provisions.","grader_hint":"The policy should not present regulator interest, group convenience, or litigation relevance as a freestanding exception."},{"rubric_item_id":"R39","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Ontario access and correction rights are presented with appropriate protection for solicitor-client privilege, confidential third-party information, and lawful refusal grounds.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Ontario access provisions.","grader_hint":"Check for a balanced right-and-exception formulation rather than an absolute promise or blanket refusal."},{"rubric_item_id":"R40","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The Ontario section states that personal information is kept only as long as necessary for identified purposes and is securely destroyed, erased, or anonymised when no longer required, subject to other record duties and holds.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Ontario retention and disposal provisions.","grader_hint":"All three elements—necessity, secure disposition, and overriding obligations—must be present."},{"rubric_item_id":"R41","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The duty to respond and cooperate with the Law Society of Ontario is distinguished from authority to publish a complaint record or circulate it freely within the combined group.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Ontario regulator-request language.","grader_hint":"The policy must preserve cooperation with lawful regulatory processes without converting that duty into a general disclosure licence."},{"rubric_item_id":"R42","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document identifies the applicable six-year retention period following completion for prescribed Ontario client-identification and verification records.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Ontario record-category schedule or legal note.","grader_hint":"Credit requires the category, six-year period, and completion-based trigger; it must not be presented as the rule for every complaint document."},{"rubric_item_id":"R43","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document identifies the separate minimum ten-year treatment applicable to prescribed Ontario financial or trust-accounting records.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Ontario record-category schedule or legal note.","grader_hint":"The policy may state the rule through a category-based schedule; it must preserve the distinction from complaint correspondence and identification records."},{"rubric_item_id":"R44","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The transfer or integration of files does not purport to extinguish confidentiality, privilege, purpose restrictions, or existing holds.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Integration and legacy-record provisions.","grader_hint":"Look for continuity of restrictions across the organisational transition."},{"rubric_item_id":"R45","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Use of service providers or cross-border systems is conditioned on purpose-limited access, contractual safeguards, security, and continuing accountability rather than treated as an unrestricted disclosure.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Third-party processing and transfer provisions.","grader_hint":"A concise control statement is sufficient; no technical security tutorial is required."},{"rubric_item_id":"R46","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The Ontario complaint route does not imply that only a defined client may raise a concern with the Law Society of Ontario.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Ontario eligibility and external-route wording.","grader_hint":"Check that non-client complainants are not incorrectly excluded while client-specific confidentiality and redress language remains distinct."},{"rubric_item_id":"R47","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The corrected policy rejects a universal seven-year period running from complaint opening and explains that no single period governs every complaint-record category in both jurisdictions.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Retention policy and change record.","grader_hint":"Credit requires an operative replacement for the draft proposition, not merely deletion of the number seven."},{"rubric_item_id":"R48","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Retention is determined by record category, jurisdiction, purpose, governing minimum period, trigger date, limitation or insurance need, and disposition or review point.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Retention framework or schedule.","grader_hint":"The framework must be usable for classification; every listed factor need not be a separate column."},{"rubric_item_id":"R49","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Legal holds, preservation notices, active proceedings, investigations, and reasonably anticipated claims suspend ordinary destruction until formally released.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Hold and preservation clause.","grader_hint":"Look for both the suspension effect and a release requirement."},{"rubric_item_id":"R50","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Where legacy classification, consent, closure status, or governing dates cannot be verified, the fallback is restricted preservation and escalation rather than assumed disclosure or routine destruction.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Legacy-transition and uncertainty provisions.","grader_hint":"This is the required defensible fallback given the unavailable file-level audit."},{"rubric_item_id":"R51","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Complaint closure is not treated as the destruction trigger for every embedded record category, and the policy accounts for records whose governing trigger is completion, creation, fiscal period, or release of a hold.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Trigger definitions and legacy applications.","grader_hint":"Check for a clear distinction between the complaint container and records within it."},{"rubric_item_id":"R52","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The policy requires authorised disposal and a record of what was destroyed or anonymised, under which rule, when, and by whom.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Disposition-control clause.","grader_hint":"Equivalent audit-record fields are acceptable if they identify the decision and accountable actor."},{"rubric_item_id":"R53","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"EW-22 is analysed under the Legal Ombudsman regime relevant to its 20 March 2023 final response, including the former outer-limit framework and the six-month final-response window, without treating expiry of an Ombudsman window as automatic authority to destroy the file.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Internal legacy decision table.","grader_hint":"The table should align the pre-1 April 2023 response with the historical rule and separately reserve the retention analysis."},{"rubric_item_id":"R54","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"EW-23 is analysed under the post-1 April 2023 Legal Ombudsman one-year framework and the six-month final-response rule, with any retention outcome kept separate from complaint eligibility.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Internal legacy decision table.","grader_hint":"Verify alignment to the 15 May and 20 July 2023 dates and preservation of the Ombudsman’s discretionary treatment."},{"rubric_item_id":"R55","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"ON-19 is split by record category: the client-identification record is assessed against its completion-based six-year rule, while the trust ledger remains subject to the longer financial-record rule rather than one complaint-level disposal date.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Internal legacy decision table.","grader_hint":"As at 30 September 2025, the table may flag the identification minimum as elapsed subject to other needs, but it must not treat the trust record as disposable on that basis."},{"rubric_item_id":"R56","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"ON-19’s missing consent status is recorded as unknown and does not support consent-based disclosure.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Internal legacy decision table and consent rule.","grader_hint":"The absence of verified consent must produce a restricted or escalated disclosure disposition, not a factual assumption."},{"rubric_item_id":"R57","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"ON-25 is placed under continued preservation with no ordinary destruction while the preservation notice lacks a release date.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Internal legacy decision table.","grader_hint":"The threatened negligence claim and unreleased notice must control despite the complaint having closed."},{"rubric_item_id":"R58","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The legacy decision table is compact, de-identified beyond the supplied matter codes, and states the governing date or uncertainty, applicable rule, present disposition, and required escalation or review trigger for each example.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Internal legacy decision table.","grader_hint":"All four matters must be individually actionable without expanding their factual narratives."},{"rubric_item_id":"R59","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The comparative analysis reproduces the supplied period and jurisdiction counts exactly, with a tolerance of ±0 complaints for every received, first-response closure, and escalation count.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Comparison table and underlying arithmetic.","grader_hint":"Verify 2024 England and Wales 80/68/12, Ontario 50/44/6, and 2025 England and Wales 100/78/22, Ontario 60/48/12."},{"rubric_item_id":"R60","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document reports total complaints rising from 130 to 160, an increase of 30 or 23.1%, and correctly reports the jurisdiction changes, with tolerances of ±0 complaints and ±0.1 percentage point.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Period-comparison calculations.","grader_hint":"England and Wales increases by 20 or 25.0%; Ontario increases by 10 or 20.0%."},{"rubric_item_id":"R61","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The escalation analysis reports the combined change from 18 to 34, an increase of 16 or 88.9%, and accurately compares jurisdiction escalation rates, with tolerances of ±0 complaints and ±0.1 percentage point.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Escalation comparison.","grader_hint":"England and Wales changes from 15.0% to 22.0%, or +7.0pp; Ontario changes from 12.0% to 20.0%, or +8.0pp."},{"rubric_item_id":"R62","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The combined first-response closure rate is shown as falling from 86.2% to 78.8%, a decrease of approximately 7.4 percentage points, with a tolerance of ±0.1 percentage point.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Closure-rate comparison.","grader_hint":"The unrounded ratios are 112/130 and 126/160; jurisdiction changes are 85.0% to 78.0% and 88.0% to 80.0%."},{"rubric_item_id":"R63","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Under the stated projection, the document keeps volumes at 100 and 60, raises each jurisdiction’s closure rate by exactly 5.0 percentage points, and derives 83 and 51 first-response closures and 17 and 9 escalations, with tolerances of ±0 records and ±0.1 percentage point.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Projection table or calculation note.","grader_hint":"The combined projection is 134 first-response closures, 26 escalations, and an 83.75% closure rate, which may be displayed as 83.8%."},{"rubric_item_id":"R64","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Counts, percentages, rounding, and the planning assumption agree across the policy, comparison table, change record, and approval note.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"All quantitative references in the document.","grader_hint":"Recalculate totals and rates and flag contradictory rounded values or a projection that silently uses a different assumption."},{"rubric_item_id":"R65","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The change explanation identifies both increased volume and a materially higher escalation share while expressly avoiding an unsupported causal claim.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Comparative narrative and approval analysis.","grader_hint":"A defensible explanation describes what changed and its operational significance, then states that the summary data do not establish why."},{"rubric_item_id":"R66","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Material legal propositions are supported by accurate, identifiable primary or official authorities, with pinpoint references where practical and a clear distinction between binding duties and guidance.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Footnotes, endnotes, source table, or concise authority references.","grader_hint":"Expected sources include the SRA Codes and Transparency Rules, Legal Ombudsman Scheme Rules, UK GDPR and Data Protection Act 2018, Ontario Rules of Professional Conduct, PIPEDA, and relevant LSO by-laws."},{"rubric_item_id":"R67","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The change record identifies the corrected blanket retention rule, corrected blanket disclosure rule, date-sensitive Legal Ombudsman wording, definition of client, legacy uncertainty control, and separation of observed and projected figures.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Change record.","grader_hint":"The record may be compact, but it must allow an approver to understand the material corrections without reconstructing them from the whole policy."},{"rubric_item_id":"R68","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Operational ownership and timing are concrete: frontline staff know the immediate intake and restriction duties, who must decide uncertain disclosure or destruction questions, when holds may be released, and when the integrated policy and schedule will be reviewed.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Roles, escalation points, and review controls.","grader_hint":"Named job functions are sufficient; do not require invented personal names. Timing must be usable rather than stated only as “periodically” or “as needed.”"},{"rubric_item_id":"R69","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The rendered document is professionally presented and accessible, with a clear hierarchy, readable tables, repeated table headings where needed, descriptive link text, and no clipped, overlapping, or illegibly dense content.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered pages and DOCX structure.","grader_hint":"Review at normal reading size and inspect whether public readers and frontline staff can scan the document reliably within the page cap."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"Ballantry Practice is integrating Candlewick Foundry’s complaints function and must publish a corrected policy for use by public readers, intake staff, and regulatory lawyers. The document must support Friday’s publication decision despite incomplete legacy classifications and insufficient time for a file-level audit.","difficulty_sources":["Aligning complaint and record rules to two jurisdictions and multiple effective dates.","Separating complaint eligibility periods from category-specific retention obligations.","Producing a defensible restricted-preservation fallback without inventing missing legacy facts.","Explaining legal controls to public and frontline readers without losing precision or becoming tutorial material.","Maintaining agreement between observed figures, derived comparisons, projections, case applications, change records, and the final publication decision within a seven-page cap."],"authoring_plan":{"solver_instance":"A senior regulatory lawyer must produce one compact DOCX combining publication-ready policy language with restricted approval material. The solution must be legally accurate, operationally executable, quantitatively consistent, and decisive despite incomplete legacy classifications.","imperfections_to_preserve":["The proposed blanket seven-year period incorrectly treats all complaint material, jurisdictions, categories, and trigger dates alike.","The proposed permission to share with any group entity or regulator whenever useful ignores confidentiality, privilege, purpose limitation, lawful authority, necessity, and role-based access.","No file-level legacy audit is available before publication.","The first legacy England and Wales matter crosses the 1 April 2023 Legal Ombudsman rule change; the second falls after it.","One Ontario file combines categories governed by different retention rules.","Consent is unverified for one Ontario matter and must remain unknown.","Another Ontario matter is closed but subject to an unreleased preservation notice.","The complaint inventory supports descriptive comparison but no causal explanation.","The public audience and legal reviewers require different levels of explanation within one short package."],"governing_content":{"england_and_wales":["Use the SRA Code of Conduct for Firms requirements for prompt, fair, free complaint handling; written client information; eight-week unresolved complaint notification; confidentiality; and cooperation with regulatory processes.","Use the SRA Transparency Rules requirement for publication of complaint procedure information.","Use the Legal Ombudsman Scheme Rules current one-year act-or-knowledge framework, six months from a compliant final response, discretion where fair and reasonable, and the 1 April 2023 transition from the former six-year or three-year knowledge framework.","Apply UK GDPR purpose limitation, data minimisation, storage limitation, lawful basis, security, access, special-category and criminal-offence safeguards, together with applicable Data Protection Act 2018 privilege and third-party restrictions.","Do not treat consent as the universal processing basis, an expired Ombudsman period as a destruction rule, or a regulator’s role as automatic authority for unrestricted disclosure."],"ontario":["Apply the Ontario Rules of Professional Conduct duty of strict confidentiality and the limited bases for authorised, permitted, or required disclosure.","Apply PIPEDA to personal information in commercial activities, including accountability, consent, limiting collection, limiting use/disclosure/retention, safeguards, access, and lawful exceptions.","Recognise that a Law Society complaint may originate from someone other than a client while preserving client-specific duties and rights.","Distinguish cooperation with the Law Society of Ontario from public or groupwide disclosure authority.","Apply the six-year completion-based rule to prescribed client-identification and verification records and the separate ten-year minimum to prescribed financial or trust-accounting records.","Do not imply that Ontario has a general private-sector provincial privacy statute equivalent to the UK GDPR or that all complaint records share one statutory retention period."],"cross_jurisdiction_controls":["Classify retention by jurisdiction, record category, purpose, trigger, applicable minimum, limitation or insurance need, active process, and hold status.","A legal hold, preservation notice, investigation, active proceeding, or reasonably anticipated claim suspends destruction until formal release.","Unknown classification, consent, closure status, or governing date produces restricted preservation and escalation, not assumed disclosure or destruction.","Complaint closure does not override a different trigger governing an embedded record category.","Group integration does not extinguish confidentiality, privilege, purpose restrictions, or holds.","Service-provider and cross-border handling remains purpose-limited, secured, contractually controlled, and accountable.","Disposition must be authorised and logged."]},"legacy_applications":{"EW-22":"Align the 20 March 2023 final response to the pre-1 April 2023 Legal Ombudsman outer-limit framework and the six-month final-response period. Do not use expiry of complaint eligibility as an automatic retention decision.","EW-23":"Apply the post-1 April 2023 one-year act-or-knowledge framework and six-month final-response period, preserving the Ombudsman’s discretion and separating eligibility from retention.","ON-19":"Separate the client-identification record from the trust ledger. As at the policy date, the six-year identification minimum following 30 June 2019 has elapsed, subject to other purposes and holds, while the trust ledger remains governed by the longer financial-record requirement. Treat consent as unknown and unavailable as a disclosure basis.","ON-25":"Preserve the record and suspend ordinary destruction because the preservation notice has no release date, notwithstanding closure of the complaint."},"quantitative_gold":{"source_relationships":["For each jurisdiction and period, complaints received equal first-response closures plus external escalations.","Prior-period totals are 130 received, 112 first-response closures, and 18 escalations.","Latest-period totals are 160 received, 126 first-response closures, and 34 escalations."],"observed_changes":["Combined received volume rises by 30, or 23.0769%.","England and Wales volume rises by 20, or 25.0%; Ontario volume rises by 10, or 20.0%.","Combined escalations rise by 16, or 88.8889%.","England and Wales escalation rate rises from 15.0% to 22.0%, or 7.0 percentage points.","Ontario escalation rate rises from 12.0% to 20.0%, or 8.0 percentage points.","Combined first-response closure rate falls from 86.1538% to 78.75%, or about 7.4038 percentage points."],"projection":["Hold volumes at 100 for England and Wales and 60 for Ontario.","Increase the latest first-response closure rate by exactly 5 percentage points in each jurisdiction.","England and Wales projects 83 first-response closures and 17 escalations.","Ontario projects 51 first-response closures and 9 escalations.","Combined projection is 134 first-response closures, 26 escalations, and an 83.75% first-response closure rate."],"interpretive_limit":"Explain that both volume and escalation share increased materially, but do not infer why from the summary inventory."},"required_vocabulary_and_provenance":["The request supplies the exact output filename, policy date, jurisdictions, legacy identifiers, factual dates, inventory figures, planning assumption, and the required term “client.”","Legal terminology and citation choices come from occupational knowledge rather than a local code list.","The document must distinguish client, complainant, regulator, service provider, group entity, consent, lawful authority, privilege, legal hold, record category, trigger date, observed result, and projection."],"intended_verdict":"Recommend publication of the corrected package on Friday. The original blanket retention and disclosure language is not publishable. Publication remains defensible without delaying for a complete audit because uncertain legacy records are restricted, preserved, and escalated under transitional controls.","space_feasibility":"The seven-page cap is achievable by concise public policy clauses, compact category and legacy tables, a short comparison, a compact change record, and a brief internal approval analysis. The rubric does not require a separate paragraph for every legal proposition."},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"primary_plus_appendix","structure":"required_sections"},"reference_files":[],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"corrected_policy","filename":"corrected_cross_jurisdiction_public_complaints_policy.docx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","description":"A publication-ready cross-jurisdiction public complaints policy with an internal legacy-record decision table, change record, comparative analysis, and publication approval note.","required":true,"constraints":["Write the file at the top level of the working directory using exactly the specified filename.","The complete document must not exceed seven rendered pages.","Apply only England and Wales and Ontario requirements in force as at 30 September 2025.","Separate publication-ready text from internal decision material with a clear page break.","End with exactly one publication recommendation as the last substantive sentence.","Do not invent facts, figures, assumptions, consents, holds, or case outcomes."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"evaluate_compliance","work_product":"decision_memo","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"closed_world"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"lawyers","sector":"professional_scientific_technical_services","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":7.5,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"hybrid","reviewer_policy":"The reference solution must be prepared or substantively reviewed by a lawyer competent in professional regulation, privacy, confidentiality, and complaints handling in both England and Wales and Ontario. A second reviewer must independently verify all calculations, date-sensitive rules, case applications, publication usability, and the single-recommendation constraint against the rendered DOCX.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["The legal position is intentionally fixed at 30 September 2025 and should not be graded against later amendments.","No underlying complaint files are supplied, so the solution must preserve and label every factual uncertainty stated in the request.","The document is a synthetic enterprise policy package and does not determine the merits of any complaint or provide advice to an actual individual."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:52:54+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:52:54+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:52:54+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:52:54+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_main/runs/5eb70c6723f66380","declared_deliverables":["corrected_cross_jurisdiction_public_complaints_policy.docx"]};
